design.md --- Text
1 # UAC lab design
2 
3 Audience: developer/operator running a local protocol simulation. Task: enroll, authenticate, issue and invoke one access grant. Tone: utilitarian. Genre: modern-minimal. Layout: workbench with a narrow identity rail and a main sequential operation panel; no marketing hero or feature tiles.
4 
5 Use system sans-serif and monospace for machine values. Palette: paper #f4f3ee, ink #202722, accent #245c45, line #d8ddd4. Spacing is 4/8/16/24/32 px. No animation except a short opacity transition. Labels remain explicit. Show simulated authenticator and deferred hardware status. Buttons have focus, disabled, busy, error and success states. No invented metrics. Responsive layout stacks the rail above the workspace below 760 px.
6 

docs/SPEC.md --- Text
1 # UAC simulation spike
2 
3 The browser creates and uses WebAuthn credentials through real navigator.credentials APIs. The authenticator is an isolated virtual CTAP2 device. Rust verifies the signatures through webauthn-rs. A signed access grant is bound to the enrolled guest credential. A server-side, single-use WebAuthn challenge is paired with the exact invocation. SQLite stores credentials, revocations and accepted operations. Challenges stay server-side and expire; process restart invalidates outstanding challenges.
4 
5 Tests: owner and guest enrollment, read grant creation, guest invocation, changed action/resource/arguments, wrong origin/RP/challenge/signature/user-verification, replay, foreign holder, expired/revoked grant, restart and duplicate registration. Live hardware is deferred by the user.
6 
7 Private keys created for the local issuer stay under data/. No production accounts, funds or physical actuators are involved. The spike uses its own versioned grant profile, not a claim of UCAN interoperability.
8 

